{"kind":"assert","text":"Ruling on request cc24b7fd, identifiers (c+d): the six other stores.\nOne rule covers them, in two cases.\n\nCase A. A store that the code names takes a new name in the new\nformat. The new code opens only the new name. It never opens, reads,\ncounts or changes the old store, and the new store starts empty.\nCase B. A store that a caller names keeps its place and holds a format\nword, as a room store does under e46dd964 item 1. A store with\ncontent and no word is refused whole, before any read of a row, and\nit stays as it is.\n\nNo old row is translated, deleted, sent again or counted against a\nnew room in either case. Old bytes stay where they are, and the reader\ncommit reads them.\n\n1. Scope index (row 04): case A. The index object takes a new fixed\n   name for format 2. `applied-genesis-v1` keeps its rows and its\n   census, unopened. The note says how the first scope of a deployment\n   enters an empty index while `SCOPE_INDEX_MODE` is `enforce`. If it\n   cannot, say so: I then deploy in `register` and return to\n   `enforce`.\n2. Site provider tokens (row 05): they live in the index object, so\n   they move with item 1. A new room mints into the new store. The\n   old reservations and ready tokens stay unread and end at their own\n   expiry. That is not a second mint for one reservation: the old\n   reservation belongs to a store that no code serves. I deploy the\n   cutover when no old read token has more than its 120 seconds left.\n3. Hosted registry and agents (rows 06 to 08).\n   a. Registry: case A. The object name takes the full Membership\n      reference, scope and genesis. A new room at an old address gets\n      a new registry.\n   b. Agent: case B. An agent object is named by an ID that a\n      credential holds. Its store takes the format word. An old agent\n      answers every entry with the retired refusal of e46dd964 item 2,\n      and its alarm does nothing. Its config, sessions, grants,\n      custody, seed and revoke debt stay untouched. The code mints no\n      replacement agent and enrols nobody again by itself.\n   c. `MCP_ENABLED` is off on every Worker, so no person meets this\n      refusal today.\n4. Local adapters that a host reuses (rows 11 and 13): case B.\n   `fileStore(dir)` writes the format word when it first sets up an\n   empty directory. A directory with content and no word is refused\n   with a named error. `reviewed-heads.json` moves to `version: 2`,\n   and a file of version 1 is refused. Neither is read for recovery or\n   for accounting under new code. The golden of NF-3 covers record\n   bytes, and the word is not a record.\n5. Page (rows 14, 15 and 18): case A. The settings slot, the Start and\n   enrollment originals and the tab list record take new storage\n   names. The new Page reads none of the old names, so the legacy key\n   migration has nothing to read: remove that path from the new code.\n   The key store holds keys and no reference, and it stays.\n6. Counter and Lending (rows 20 to 22): case A. Their browser\n   databases take new names. No capacity is shared with an old row,\n   and no default or address-only slot can load one.\n\nA person who returns with an old link gets `room-retired` from the\nWorker. The old browser rows stay in the browser, unread. Say that in\nthe note and in one sentence of the release text.\n\nThe rows that you marked as needing nothing need nothing.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}